.TH VDR-SUITE-BACKEND-AGENT-AUTOSTART 8 "October 2026" "VDR-Suite" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
vdr-suite-backend-agent-autostart \- enable an already enrolled Backend Agent at boot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vdr-suite-backend-agent-autostart
.RB ( --check | --enable | --setup-status | --prepare-enrollment | --activate-local | --installer-onboard )
.SH DESCRIPTION
A protected post-enrollment setup step. The experimental package installs
this command but deliberately does not invoke it in
.BR postinst .
A fresh, unenrolled Agent is not auto-enabled or started by package installation.
.PP
.B --setup-status
reports whether the local VDR and daemon are running and whether the
Backend Agent package/configuration, identity or one-time enrollment is
present. It does not read credential contents, initialize the database,
create enrollment, start a service, or change boot policy. The output
.B LOCAL_BACKEND_SETUP
is a preliminary first-run state classification, not evidence of an
authenticated, connected Agent or a finished installation. The eventual
installer-owned setup path will consume this status internally; end users
should not need this command.
.PP
.B --check
validates the current installed systemd service, non-secret configuration,
protected local identity and the existing Backend Agent administration status.
It requires an active
.B vdr:vdr
service, trusted HTTPS Control Plane, consumed enrollment package, current
authenticated online lease and recent Backend health observation.
The verification uses the existing local
.B vdr-suite-backend-agent-admin --status
command, not direct SQL. The status utility still performs its own normal
repository/schema initialization; this is not a database migration step.
Any missing, stale or unexpected fact fails closed. This helper currently targets
a same-host Control Plane and Agent: a remote Agent with no local Control
Plane status database must not bypass the guard. Remote activation requires
a separately reviewed remote readiness integration.
.PP
.B --prepare-enrollment
is an internal, root-only step for a trusted first-run installer on a
single local VDR. It uses the configured Backend ID and existing
.B vdr-suite-backend-agent-enroll
utility to create one protected enrollment package in the pre-existing
Agent state directory, then grants the packaged Agent account access to
that package. It requires a configured trusted HTTPS Control Plane, the
existing local Control Plane database, an inactive Agent and no existing
identity or enrollment. It never starts services, overwrites an
identity, issues a second enrollment during retry, or prints the token.
Creating the package is not the same as completing Agent enrollment.
The installer must arrange service start and successful Agent readback
as a later separate step.
.PP
.B --activate-local
is an installer-owned continuation after an enrollment package has already
been prepared. On the reviewed same-host installation, it requires the
existing channel observation adapter (`channels-conf`) and observation
domain (`channels`) to be configured against the actual local VDR
channel source. It starts the inactive Agent once through the existing
systemd unit, requires authenticated current Agent and backend-health
readback, then checks a matching fresh channel observation cursor with
nonempty channel facts. Only then does it enable boot startup, reusing
the existing post-enrollment activation checks. It does not restart VDR
or the daemon, replace identities or attempt a second enrollment.
.PP
This command is not an interactive end-user setup path, and does not
discover or rewrite the MLD-specific channel file location. That
distribution configuration and the browser/installer trigger remain
separate unfinished work.
.PP
.B --installer-onboard
combines the already implemented local first-run actions for the trusted
distribution installer. It requires the existing packaged local service,
a configured real HTTPS Control Plane, the observed VDR channel source,
and the running VDR and daemon. On fresh state it prepares one enrollment
and activates the Agent. With an existing pending enrollment it resumes
activation without issuing another token. With an existing protected identity it checks the reviewed package
unit and non-secret identity fields, resumes an inactive Agent only after
these match, and requires authenticated Backend and Channel readback
without re-enrollment before enabling boot startup. Unknown units
and mismatched identities are never started.
The MLD package recipe must invoke this entrypoint at the correct
install/first-boot point. Normal Ubuntu/yaVDR installations do not invoke
it, and the frontend never runs it.
.PP
.B --enable
requires root and the same successful preflight immediately before enabling
the already running service for future boots. It invokes
.B systemctl enable vdr-suite-backend-agent.service
without
.B --now
and never starts, stops or restarts VDR, the Agent or the daemon.
It does not enroll a new identity, rotate or revoke credentials, alter
existing Timers or replay historical Agent commands.
.PP
The Ubuntu/yaVDR package deliberately does not invoke this enrollment
entrypoint. A trusted MLD first-run recipe may invoke it automatically
without asking the browser or operator to run root commands.
On systems with custom systemd unit drop-ins, unexpected binary locations,
masked units or unverified state, perform a separate operator review instead
of bypassing the safety checks.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --check
Safe preflight; never change systemd startup links.
.TP
.B --setup-status
Read-only local VDR/Agent first-run classification; no enrollment or service changes.
.TP
.B --prepare-enrollment
Installer-only local first-run package preparation. Mutates local Agent
enrollment state; it does not start services.
.TP
.B --activate-local
Installer-only Agent startup, authenticated readiness, fresh Channel
observation and future boot activation. Blocks when not ready.
.TP
.B --installer-onboard
Root-only repeat-safe MLD package handoff; not a browser endpoint.
.TP
.B --enable
Enable boot startup after a fresh successful preflight. No service restart.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero for successful check/enable, nonzero if unsafe or incomplete.
Output never includes credentials or enrollment tokens.
.SH SECURITY
Identity state is reused in
.I /var/lib/vdr-suite/backend-agent/identity
and is never recreated. The enrolled device must have a current Backend Agent
heartbeat and backend health observation before startup is enabled.
A later reboot acceptance must separately verify that same identity and
current lease/provider facts/epoch.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vdr-suite-backend-agent (8),
.BR vdr-suite-backend-agent-admin (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
